## Actual five-JAR closure

Whole-JAR SHA/size identities are in `kafka-artifact-review.md` and the report.
The following embedded statements agree with those pinned filenames:

| Exact JAR | Actual embedded version evidence | License evidence |
| --- | --- | --- |
| kafka-clients-4.4.0.jar | kafka/kafka-version.properties:4.4.0, commit1156b2752aaa28ea | Original LICENSE Apache2.0; Kafka NOTICE plus shaded Commons Net Apache2.0 LICENSE/NOTICE |
| lz4-java-1.11.2.jar | Manifest Bundle-Version1.11.2; Maven at.yawk.lz4:lz4-java:1.11.2 | Manifest/POM Apache2.0, matching image distribution list |
| slf4j-api-1.7.36.jar | Manifest Implementation-Version1.7.36; Maven org.slf4j:slf4j-api:1.7.36 | Image licenses/slf4j-MIT, original QOS.ch MIT text |
| snappy-java-1.1.10.7.jar | Manifest Implementation/Specification/Bundle-Version1.1.10.7 | Manifest Apache2.0, matching image distribution list |
| zstd-jni-1.5.7-17.jar | Manifest Implementation/Specification-Version1.5.7-17; OSGi Bundle-Version1.5.7.17 | Manifest BSD2-clause; image licenses/zstd-jni-BSD-2-clause original Luben Karavelov text |

These are artifact statements, not independently reproduced builds. The LZ4
POM introductory comment describes reproducing1.8.0, while its actual project
version and manifest both say1.11.2; do not use that stale comment as provenance.
Its declared dependencies are test-scoped and do not authorize JUnit/Jazzer
acquisition or Maven execution. SLF4J's POM has build-plugin configuration, not
additional runtime dependencies. No POM was parsed as executable configuration.

The exact external oracle classpath proposal remains these five JARs only,
plus the compiled oracle class directory. No wildcard, Kafka distribution
plugin/logging directory, Maven resolver or extra downloaded SDK is needed.
This bounded offline compilation and native run must prove that proposed closure;
metadata inspection alone cannot prove runtime linkage.

There is additional code inside the pinned Kafka JAR:204 shaded Commons Net
classes,735 shaded protobuf classes and325 shaded OpenTelemetry proto classes.
Commons Net's embedded Maven properties identify3.13.0. The image distribution
list names protobuf-java3.25.5 and opentelemetry-proto1.3.2-alpha, but the selected
Kafka metadata does not independently bind those exact versions to shaded class
bytes. They are included in the JAR identity and cannot be omitted from its
attribution review or described as absent because there are only five files.

An actual attribution discrepancy remains: image file
`opt/kafka/licenses/protobuf-java-BSD-3-clause` (`kafka-6-2635.data`, SHA
`42e972d1d5bd3c02caa548c08ad0ea27f722bfb753802fc9effbcd723ad8a5cc`)
contains a2023 original-author notice and a JLine non-endorsement clause.
It contains no Google/protobuf-specific copyright attribution. This review records
the bytes, rather than silently correcting upstream attribution or claiming legal
closure. The separate immutable upstream attribution investigation below supports
isolated test use; public redistribution needs its own decision.
The four full selected Kafka LICENSE/NOTICE files are Apache2.0/Kafka/Commons Net
texts; none contains Google/protobuf attribution. All legal/notice-like member
names in the admitted index were selected, so no additional matching named JAR
notice remains hidden behind the unselected-class limitation.

Bounded curl fallback subsequently retained the exact Kafka source commit's
build/dependency declarations. They verify all four external runtime dependencies,
three explicit shaded roots and pinned versions, agreeing with the artifact data.
The source's protobuf notice is byte-identical to the image's wrong JLine notice.
Separate immutable upstream protobuf3.25.5 source now supplies Google's BSD3
LICENSE, and immutable OTel source supplies its Apache2.0 LICENSE. Full URL/hash,
tag-to-commit chains, bounds and limits are in `kafka-attribution-receipt.md`.
This supplements attribution for isolated testing; it does not modify or green
the original image, prove reproduced builds, or claim correct upstream packaging.

## Services, logging and native exposure

All five physical member indexes contain no manifest Class-Path, Java signature
entries, SLF4J StaticLoggerBinder or SLF4JServiceProvider. SLF4J's selected POM
explicitly removes its dummy binding classes. Do not add a logging binding to
silence expected stderr warnings or let them enter binary oracle stdout.

The only service file is Kafka's ConfigProvider descriptor. Its actual providers
are FileConfigProvider, DirectoryConfigProvider and EnvVarConfigProvider.
They can access files/environment if enabled by client configuration. The frozen
oracle constructs its own Properties, passes byte serializers directly and has
no caller configuration passthrough. The launcher must not forward credentials,
proxies, CLASSPATH, JAVA_TOOL_OPTIONS, JDK_JAVA_OPTIONS, _JAVA_OPTIONS, plugin or
config-provider settings. Do not claim that a service descriptor proves providers
are never loaded; a source/runtime check of the exact client path remains useful.
No annotation-processor service was found. Compile with `-proc:none` nonetheless.

The codec indexes contain54 native members:9LZ4,27Snappy,18Zstd, including Linux
aarch64 and other platforms. Snappy has an OSGi Bundle-Activator and native-code
manifest; Zstd declares optional OSGi imports. These do not require OSGi in the
proposed plain Java classpath. They are executable potential, not proven safe
payloads. The uncompressed oracle requests compression=none and the isolated
topic must contain only reviewed uncompressed records. No codec/native loading
or musl compatibility claim follows. The original compressed class/native member
bytes were neither decompressed nor CRC-verified; only whole-JAR SHA anchors them.
No cryptographic signature or static bytecode audit was performed.

## Recommendation and finite next recipe

The source/attribution investigation now supports preparing the narrow offline
compiler gate below with original notices and upstream attribution supplement
retained. Root should review the frozen launcher as the ordinary next test gate;
no additional user dependency approval is needed. No acquired code has executed.
Do not yet
call the full broker image or compressed codecs qualified. The source commit
matches Kafka's embedded abbreviated commit, but the prior RC4 promotion versus
final source-to-binary equivalence limitation remains. Exact immutable image
identity is useful test provenance, not a complete supply-chain attestation.

The Kafka JRE release states Temurin25.0.4.1+1/musl/aarch64; compiler JDK states
Temurin25.0.5+8/glibc/aarch64. Their base GPL2 LICENSE, Classpath clarification
and assembly-exception texts match exactly, with per-component legal references
resolved only through the separate logical supplement. Run each complete pinned
image in its own environment; never transplant the glibc JDK into Alpine. These
facts support trying `--release 17`; actual compilation and runtime remain unproven.

1. Freeze the two Java source hashes, five JAR identities, inventory, supplement
   and current artifact report. Recheck free disk before immutable image
   acquisition; allow only the exact arm64 references in `kafka-oracle-recipe.md`
   and verify actual child/config/platform/declared volumes. Account compressed
   Docker storage separately from the1500MiB expanded retention envelope and
  128MiB research reserve. Last data receipt had3073466368B host free, not proof
   that the next pull fits. No mutable fallback, unrelated pruning or parallel job.
2. Create a stopped compiler container, network none/no host mounts or ports,
   memory=swap512MiB,CPU1/PID128,cap-dropALL/no-new-privileges. Override the image
   entrypoint, copy readonly source/JARs into `/work`, and write only `/tmp/classes`.
   Use literal five-JAR paths: `javac -J-Xms16m -J-Xmx128m
   -J-XX:ActiveProcessorCount=1 -proc:none --release 17 -cp <five-literal-paths>
   -d /tmp/classes /work/KafkaOracle.java /work/CrcOracle.java`.
   A frozen owner supplies the reviewed fixed mkdir/exec command,60s external
   watchdog and1MiB diagnostic cap. Do not start a broker during compilation.
3. Preserve terminal/resource/OOM/log evidence before removal. Copy only bounded
   ordinary generated class files from the stopped compiler using a reviewed
   physical archive parser; cap total4MiB/count128, reject aliases/extensions and
   unexpected paths, hash every class. Retire and confirm that exact container.
   A compile pass is not permission to start Java or a broker.
4. After separate native-owner review, Testcontainers alone owns one pinned
   Kafka container, readiness, labelled storage and cleanup. Proposed ceiling:
   memory=swap1GiB,CPU1/PID128; broker heap256MiB and one auxiliary JVM heap128MiB
   at a time inside that same container. This tight PID/resource profile is
   unproven; inspect real failure before proposing any increase. No companion
   JVM container may run beside it. Replace all three image volumes with explicit
   owned disposable storage whose actual extent/retirement is recorded.
5. Preload exact classes/JARs and original binary inputs with stopped-container
   copy. Owned isolated networking publishes only a dynamic loopback-bound test
   listener; controller/internal/oracle listeners stay internal. No account or
   ambient authentication. Readiness and topic CLI run serially with128MiB heap;
   one topic/partition, auto-create disabled, explicit CreateTime, uncompressed
   records and bounded request/log data. Bound total broker life180s, readiness
  90s, each CLI30s and oracle45s; preserve partial outcomes on timeout.
6. Run the independent literal probe and Java oracle in the pre-reviewed order,
   with finite binary stdin/EOF and stdout4MiB/stderr1MiB caps. Compare original
   full bytes in both directions, null/empty fields, ordered duplicate headers,
   actual partitions/offsets/timestamps and Java CRC32C. Retain original bounded
   wire responses and correlations. No candidate-generated expectation fixtures.
   SDK poll limits do not prove candidate raw-page overflow refusal. Keep that
   separate original-wire assertion and unknown-append outcome checks.
7. Stop once the bounded sequence settles; retain broker state/logs, all native
   outputs and owned-volume extents before exact removal and confirmation.
   No successful stage silently approves production Kafka code, runtime SDK use,
   arbitrary broker auth, compression or public redistribution.

This recipe requires a frozen compiler/native launcher source review before
execution. The existing Python artifact launcher does not implement these JVM
stages and must not be repurposed by changing its frozen sources or modes.
